## Tutorials

❮ Java Keywords

Thetrykeyword creates a try...catch statement.

Thetrystatement allows you to define a block of code to be 
tested for errors while it is being executed.

Thecatchstatement allows you to define a block of code to 
be executed, if an error occurs in the try block.

Read more about exceptions in ourJava Try..Catch Tutorial.

❮ Java Keywords

### Code Examples

```
Example
If an error occur, use try...catch to catch the error and execute some code to handle it:
try {
  int[] myNumbers = {1, 2, 3};
  System.out.println(myNumbers[10]);
} catch (Exception e) {
  System.out.println("Something went wrong.");
}
Try it Yourself »
```

## Tutorials

❮ Java Keywords

Thevarkeyword allows a variable to be initialized without having to declare its type. The type of the variable depends on the type of the data that is being assigned to it.

Thevarkeyword was introduced in Java 10.

Read more about variables in ourJava Variables Tutorial.

❮ Java Keywords

### Code Examples

```
Example
Use
var
to declare variables:
public class Main {
  public int test = 10;

  public static void main(String[] args) {
    var x = 5;
    var main = new Main();
    System.out.println(x);
    System.out.println(main.test);
  }
}
Try it Yourself »
```

